  "title": "Your first model: learn a rule from data",
  "difficulty": "easy",
  "packages": [],
  "concept": "A model is a rule learned from past examples instead of written by hand",
  "task": "In normal programming *you* write the rule. In machine learning the computer *learns* the rule from past data.\nWrite `learn_average(past_rain)` that takes a list of daily rainfall values (in mm) and returns their average. This average is what our tiny 'model' has learned.\nThen write `is_heavy_rain(today, average)` that returns `True` if `today` is **more than** 1.5 times the average, otherwise `False`.\nExample: `learn_average([10, 20, 30])` returns `20.0`, so `is_heavy_rain(35, 20.0)` is `True` (35 > 30) and `is_heavy_rain(25, 20.0)` is `False`.\nPress Run to see what your model learns from the Mumbai data, then watch the checks turn green.",
  "starter": "# Mumbai rainfall (mm) for the last 7 days of July\npast_rain = [12, 30, 8, 45, 20, 15, 10]\n\ndef learn_average(past_rain):\n    \"\"\"Return the average of the list: the sum divided by how many values there are.\"\"\"\n    # your code here\n    pass\n\ndef is_heavy_rain(today, average):\n    \"\"\"Return True if today's rain is more than 1.5 times the average, else False.\"\"\"\n    # your code here\n    pass\n\n# Try it: press Run and read the output\nmodel = learn_average(past_rain)\nprint(\"My model learned the average:\", model)\nprint(\"Is 40 mm heavy rain?\", is_heavy_rain(40, model))\n",
  "solution": "# Mumbai rainfall (mm) for the last 7 days of July\npast_rain = [12, 30, 8, 45, 20, 15, 10]\n\ndef learn_average(past_rain):\n    \"\"\"Return the average of the list: the sum divided by how many values there are.\"\"\"\n    return sum(past_rain) / len(past_rain)\n\ndef is_heavy_rain(today, average):\n    \"\"\"Return True if today's rain is more than 1.5 times the average, else False.\"\"\"\n    # The comparison itself is already True or False, so we can return it directly\n    return today > 1.5 * average\n\n# Try it: press Run and read the output\nmodel = learn_average(past_rain)\nprint(\"My model learned the average:\", model)          # 20.0\nprint(\"Is 40 mm heavy rain?\", is_heavy_rain(40, model))  # True, because 40 > 30\n",
  "tests": "check(\"learn_average([10, 20, 30]) returns 20.0\", lambda: close(learn_average([10, 20, 30]), 20.0), \"Add the numbers with sum() and divide by how many there are with len().\")\ncheck(\"learn_average learns 20.0 from the Mumbai week\", lambda: close(learn_average([12, 30, 8, 45, 20, 15, 10]), 20.0), \"sum([12, 30, 8, 45, 20, 15, 10]) is 140 and there are 7 days.\")\ncheck(\"learn_average([5]) returns 5\", lambda: close(learn_average([5]), 5), \"The average of a single number is that number.\")\ncheck(\"is_heavy_rain(35, 20) is True\", lambda: is_heavy_rain(35, 20) == True, \"1.5 times 20 is 30, and 35 is more than 30.\")\ncheck(\"is_heavy_rain(25, 20) is False\", lambda: is_heavy_rain(25, 20) == False, \"Make sure you return False (not None) when the rain is not heavy.\")\ncheck(\"exactly 1.5 times the average (30 vs 20) is NOT heavy\", lambda: is_heavy_rain(30, 20) == False, \"The rule says *more than*, so use > and not >=.\")\ncheck(\"new data teaches a new rule: 35 mm is normal if the average is 50\", lambda: is_heavy_rain(35, learn_average([40, 50, 60])) == False, \"Feed the output of learn_average into is_heavy_rain. With an average of 50 the limit becomes 75.\")",
